  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDry conditions with above normal temperatures persist through \nthe weekend. This will exacerbate ongoing water resource drought\nconditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nDeep layered ridging builds continues today, keeping the area \ndry through due to the subsidence under the ridge. Friday \nafternoon a cold front passes through from the north, lowering \ntemperatures slightly Friday night and Saturday. No \nprecipitation is anticipated with the frontal passage.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nRain is likely especially over Downeast Maine Sunday night\nand Monday. Enough rain to make a small dent in the drought is\npossible.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nRain is looking more likely for Sunday night/Monday, especially\nDowneast. This will be coming from a strengthening surface low\npressure/Noreaster tracking northeast just off the New England\ncoast, ahead of an approaching vigorous upper level trough.\n\nThere are still some pretty substantial disagreements among\nmodels/ensembles regarding the strength of the Noreaster and \nhow far north/west it tracks. The further north/west it tracks\nand the stronger the low pressure is, the more rain falls for \nmore of Northern/Eastern Maine. If the low pressure tracks\nfurther south/east and/or is weaker, less rain would fall and\ncould miss the area entirely. \n\nThe general tendency is for a stronger/more NW track that would\nbring decent rain at least to Downeast. Right now, we are\nthinking about a 50 percent chance of at least 1 inch of rain\nDowneast, with a 30 percent chance of at least 2 inches. A band\nof heavy rain is possible to the NW of the low track, and this\nhas a decent chance of impacting Downeast. Even with this\nheavier rain scenario, flooding is unlikely except for localized\nareas with leaf-clogged drains. The chance of Downeast missing \nout on rain entirely is about 15 percent. Rain amounts will drop\noff the further NW you go, with a less than 40 percent chance \nof at least 0.50 inch rain in Northern Maine.\n\nThe incoming upper level trough from the NW is unlikely to phase\ncompletely with the Noreaster, which should keep the air warm\nenough for just rain. That said, much colder air is very likely\nto pour in Monday night behind the system, and there is the\npotential for a flash freeze of any remaining wet roads. This\ndoes not appear likely at this point, but it is a possibility.\n\nPeeking further ahead, fairly quiet but cold weather is likely \nthrough Wednesday, but we will have to keep an eye out for \npotentially active weather Thanksgiving through the next \nweekend, which could include snow. Confidence is extremely low \nat this point for any single weather system.",
